  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\n- Breezy and warm today into Sunday\n\nWarm air advection showers continue to bring light rain early this\nmorning through central lower Michigan. A ridge of high pressure\nwill build over the Great Lakes and southern Canada today as warm\nair advection continues to stream into the area. As the warm air \nmixes out the inversion, expect gusty winds, upwards of 25 mph, \nthis afternoon. \n 850 mb temps of +15C will mix down bringing temps upwards of 15 \nto 20 degrees above normal, peaking in the upper 70s to \npotentially the low 80s. \n \n- Warm weather daytime Monday; remnants of Isaias shift east\n \n The warming trend will continue through Monday. Sunday temps will\nbe slightly cooler due to the cloudy skies from the fringes from\nthe remnants of Isaias. \n Ensembles continue to shows Isaias track shift to the southeast,\nwith it moving through the Mid Atlantic early Monday. \nPart of the reason this is occuring is due to a positively tilted\nridge that will continue warm air advection that will keep max \ntemperatures 10 to 20 degrees above normal through daytime Monday\n  \n- Pattern change Tues; strong system through latter half of week\n \n Models are showing a flattening of the ridge Monday night into \nTuesday with a more zonal upper level flow. Mid to low level low \npressure will move through Michigan into Tuesday with a strong \nmoisture boundary extending from New York, through the Great Lakes\nand into the Upper Mid West. The best moisture late Monday into \nTuesday will remain to the north. However, can't rule out some \nscattered showers Monday night into Tuesday, especially as a \ndeepening trough swings through daytime Tuesday. There should be \nenough instability for a chance of storms with this system. EC \nensembles continue to show strong anomalous moisture Monday night\ninto Tuesday with PWATS of 1.25 to 1.5 inches over the Lower \npeninsula. \n   \n Expect a more tumultuous pattern through the latter half of next\nweek. There is some question on timing of the incoming system. \nSome of the ensembles vary on where the upper level pattern will \nbe. Latest EC trends have backed off on how deep the moisture\nadvects into MI. Though there is a tilted trough that could bring\na strong frontal pattern through the end of the week. All that \nsaid, there is the potential for showers and storms through the \nlatter half of the week, with the potential for a severe weather \nevent given the strong flow, copious moisture and warm air out \nahead of the system.",
